  abstract = {Damage to the basal ganglia (BG) can produce diverse symptoms, such as neglect, abulia, akinesia, chorea, and tremor. Accordingly, these nuclei have been assigned diverse functions, but there is still no model that can explain the full range of BG contributions to behavior. Here I propose a new model of the BG based on the principles of hierarchical control. According to this model, the cerebral cortex and BG implement transition control, in particular the rate of change in various perceptual configurations. Each closed loop controller receives perceptual inputs representing the current rate of change and reference signals representing the desired rate. The BG outputs, in turn, generate transition errors to adjust the reference signals of position controllers in the midbrain and brainstem. The cortico-BG network also implements a special functional mode unique to the transition level of the control hierarchy, namely imagination. The imagination mode is the basis for planning, simulation, and working memory.},
